<!-- Main -->
<div class="main">
  <div class="lecture-header">
    <div class="lecture-eyebrow">Lecture 10</div>
    <h2>Latent Diffusion &amp; Stable Diffusion</h2>
    <p class="lecture-desc">Why pixel-space diffusion breaks at high resolution, how VAEs compress images into a small latent space, and the architecture behind Stable Diffusion.</p>
    <div class="lecture-tags">
      <span class="lecture-tag">VAE</span>
      <span class="lecture-tag">Latent Space</span>
      <span class="lecture-tag">Stable Diffusion</span>
      <span class="lecture-tag">Scaling</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <nav class="lecture-nav-top">
    <a class="prev" href="lecture09.html">&#8592; Classifier-Free Guidance</a>
    <a class="next" href="lecture11.html">Prediction Targets &amp; Advanced Schedules &#8594;</a>
  </nav>

  <article class="lecture-article">





    <p class="lecture-roadmap" style="position: relative;">The diffusion models we've discussed so far work on smaller images, but real-world generation happens at high resolutions such as 512×512 and beyond. Pixel-space diffusion has difficulty scaling to this size, because attention costs explode quadratically with resolution. The solution: compress images into a small latent space with a VAE, then run diffusion in that latent space. This is the architecture behind Stable Diffusion, and it's what made high-resolution generation practical.</p>


    <div class="callout prereq" style="position: relative;">
      <div class="callout-label" style="position: relative;">This Lecture Is For You If</div>
      <ul>
        <li style="position: relative;">You want to understand how diffusion scales to high-resolution images</li>
        <li style="position: relative;">You know what autoencoders are, or are ready to learn</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <!-- Section 1: The Resolution Bottleneck -->
    <h2 id="resolution-bottleneck" style="position: relative;"><span class="section-number">10.1</span> The Resolution Bottleneck</h2>

    <p style="position: relative;">Everything we've built so far works well on small images like 28×28 for MNIST or 32x32 for CIFAR-10. But the pixel-space diffusion paradigm hits challenges at higher resolutions. Why is that?</p>

    <p style="position: relative;">The pixel count at different resolutions tells the story:</p>

    <ul>
      <li style="position: relative;"><strong>28×28×1</strong> (grayscale): 784 values</li>
      <li style="position: relative;"><strong>32×32×3</strong> (CIFAR-10): 3,072 values</li>
      <li style="position: relative;"><strong>256×256×3</strong>: 196,608 values</li>
      <li style="position: relative;"><strong>512×512×3</strong>: 786,432 values</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="position: relative;">A U-Net handling 784 values (28×28) runs comfortably. But at 512×512, it's processing <strong>786K pixel values per denoising step</strong>, and we may need hundreds of steps. The model size isn't the bottleneck; it's the <em>feature map sizes</em> flowing through the network.</p>

    <h3 id="attention-cost" style="position: relative;">The Attention Problem</h3>

    <p style="position: relative;">Recall from Lecture 5 that attention is <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>W</mi><msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O((H \times W)^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.0278em;">O</span><span class="mopen">((</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.0813em;">H</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1.0641em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.1389em;">W</span><span class="mclose"><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.8141em;"><span class="" style="top: -3.063em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span></span> in spatial dimension. Let's run the numbers:</p>

    <ul>
      <li style="position: relative;">At <strong>32×32</strong>: attention operates on 1,024 tokens → ~1M operations per layer. Totally fine.</li>
      <li style="position: relative;">At <strong>256×256</strong>: attention operates on 65,536 tokens → ~4 billion operations per layer. Massively greater.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="position: relative;">You could avoid attention at high resolutions and rely only on convolutions, but convolutions are local operators which lose the <strong>global context</strong>&nbsp;from attention blocks that makes our generated images coherent. A face might have perfectly rendered eyes but mismatched ears, because the receptive field of convolutions isn't large enough to coordinate distant features.</p>

    

    <p style="position: relative;">You could also try progressive generation: train a low-resolution model, then train a separate super-resolution model. But this requires multiple models, multiple training runs, and information loss at each stage.</p>

    <div class="callout insight" style="position: relative;">
      <div class="callout-label" style="position: relative;">Key Insight</div>
      The fundamental bottleneck is that pixel space is <strong>too large</strong> for the operations we need. Rather than making the operations cheaper, we compress the space itself.
    </div>

    <!-- Section 2: VAE Primer -->
    <h2 id="vae-primer" style="position: relative;"><span class="section-number">10.2</span>&nbsp;Review: Variational Autoencoders (VAEs)</h2>

    <p style="position: relative;">Since compressing the space is the solution, we need a tool for the job: the variational autoencoder. We present a compact treatment here to understand what it does and why it works.</p>

    

    <h3 id="encoder-decoder" style="position: relative;">Encoder and Decoder</h3>

    <p style="position: relative;">A VAE has two halves:</p>

    <p style="position: relative;">The <strong>encoder</strong> <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.0576em;">E</span></span></span></span></span> maps an image <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span></span></span></span></span> to a lower-dimensional latent representation <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.044em;">z</span></span></span></span></span>. Commonly, it's a series of convolutional layers with downsampling (stride-2 convolutions or pooling); more modern architectures use ViT- or attention-based blocks as well.</p>

    <p style="position: relative;">Crucially, the encoder doesn't output a single point in latent space; it outputs two things: a <strong>mean</strong> <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.625em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">μ</span></span></span></span></span> and a <strong>log-variance</strong> <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log \sigma^2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1.0085em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mop">lo<span style="margin-right: 0.0139em;">g</span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.0359em;">σ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.8141em;"><span class="" style="top: -3.063em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> for a Gaussian distribution over latent codes.</p>

    <p style="position: relative;">The <strong>decoder</strong> <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.0278em;">D</span></span></span></span></span> maps a latent code <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.044em;">z</span></span></span></span></span> back to pixel space, producing a reconstructed image <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6944em;"></span><span class="mord accent"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.6944em;"><span class="" style="top: -3em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span></span><span class="" style="top: -3em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="accent-body" style="left: -0.2222em;"><span class="mord">^</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>. It's the mirror of the encoder: convolutional layers with upsampling (transposed convolutions or interpolation followed by convolution).</p>

    <p style="position: relative;">A typical compression ratio: <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>256</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>→</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256 \times 256 \times 3 \to 32 \times 32 \times 4</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">256</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">256</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">3</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">→</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">32</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">32</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">4</span></span></span></span></span>, which is a <strong>48× compression</strong> in the number of values (196,608 down to 4,096).</p>

    <p style="position: relative;">The "variational" in the name comes from training: the VAE loss combines a reconstruction term (how well the decoder recovers the original image) with a KL regularization term that pushes the encoder's output distribution toward a standard Gaussian. This regularization is what gives the latent space its useful properties.</p>

    <!-- VAE architecture diagram -->
    <div style="max-width: 70%; margin: 32px auto; background: #0f1621; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px 24px 16px; overflow: hidden;">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 700 280" style="width: 100%; height: auto; display: block;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <defs>
          <marker id="arrow-flow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto">
            <path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9z" fill="#6b7280"/>
          </marker>
        </defs>

        <!-- Dashed line: x ≈ x̂ -->
        <line x1="80" y1="32" x2="620" y2="32" stroke="#6b7280" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="6,4" opacity="0.5"/>
        <text x="350" y="24" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6b7280" font-family="'IBM Plex Mono', monospace" font-size="13" opacity="0.7">x &#8776; x&#x302;</text>

        <!-- 1. Input image x -->
        <text x="80" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b8ad98" font-family="'Source Sans 3', sans-serif" font-size="12">Input</text>
        <rect x="58" y="70" width="44" height="120" rx="4" fill="rgba(74, 222, 128, 0.12)" stroke="#4ade80" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <text x="80" y="136" text-anchor="middle" fill="#4ade80" font-family="'IBM Plex Mono', monospace" font-size="16" font-weight="bold">x</text>

        <!-- Arrow 1 -->
        <line x1="110" y1="130" x2="148" y2="130" stroke="#6b7280" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arrow-flow)"/>

        <!-- 2. Encoder trapezoid (wide left, narrow right) -->
        <text x="218" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b8ad98" font-family="'Source Sans 3', sans-serif" font-size="12">Encoder</text>
        <polygon points="155,75 280,95 280,165 155,185" fill="rgba(45, 212, 191, 0.1)" stroke="#2dd4bf" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
        <text x="210" y="136" text-anchor="middle" fill="#2dd4bf" font-family="'IBM Plex Mono', monospace" font-size="18" font-weight="bold">E</text>

        <!-- Arrow 2 -->
        <line x1="288" y1="130" x2="318" y2="130" stroke="#6b7280" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arrow-flow)"/>

        <!-- 3. Latent z (small square, accent/rose) -->
        <text x="350" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f97316" font-family="'Source Sans 3', sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="600">Bottleneck</text>
        <rect x="325" y="105" width="50" height="50" rx="4" fill="rgba(244, 114, 182, 0.15)" stroke="#f472b6" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <text x="350" y="136" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f472b6" font-family="'IBM Plex Mono', monospace" font-size="16" font-weight="bold">z</text>
        <text x="350" y="175" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b8ad98" font-family="'IBM Plex Mono', monospace" font-size="11">&#956;, &#963;</text>

        <!-- Arrow 3 -->
        <line x1="383" y1="130" x2="413" y2="130" stroke="#6b7280" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arrow-flow)"/>

        <!-- 4. Decoder trapezoid (narrow left, wide right) -->
        <text x="482" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b8ad98" font-family="'Source Sans 3', sans-serif" font-size="12">Decoder</text>
        <polygon points="420,95 545,75 545,185 420,165" fill="rgba(96, 165, 250, 0.1)" stroke="#60a5fa" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
        <text x="490" y="136" text-anchor="middle" fill="#60a5fa" font-family="'IBM Plex Mono', monospace" font-size="18" font-weight="bold">D</text>

        <!-- Arrow 4 -->
        <line x1="553" y1="130" x2="590" y2="130" stroke="#6b7280" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arrow-flow)"/>

        <!-- 5. Reconstructed image x̂ -->
        <text x="620" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b8ad98" font-family="'Source Sans 3', sans-serif" font-size="12">Reconstruction</text>
        <rect x="598" y="70" width="44" height="120" rx="4" fill="rgba(96, 165, 250, 0.12)" stroke="#60a5fa" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <text x="620" y="136" text-anchor="middle" fill="#60a5fa" font-family="'IBM Plex Mono', monospace" font-size="16" font-weight="bold">x&#x302;</text>

        <!-- Annotation below bottleneck -->
        <text x="350" y="220" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6b7280" font-family="'Source Sans 3', sans-serif" font-size="11">Compressed latent representation</text>

        <!-- Thin bracket line connecting annotation to z -->
        <line x1="350" y1="190" x2="350" y2="210" stroke="#6b7280" stroke-width="0.75" opacity="0.4"/>
      </svg>
      <p style="margin: 12px 0 0; font-size: 13px; color: #6b7280; line-height: 1.5; text-align: center;">The VAE compresses high-dimensional input <em>x</em> into a compact latent code <em>z</em>, then reconstructs <em>x&#x302;</em> &approx; <em>x</em>. The bottleneck forces the network to learn efficient representations.</p>
    </div>

    <h3 id="latent-space" style="position: relative;">The Latent Space</h3>

    <p style="position: relative;">What does the latent space actually look like? Because of that KL regularization pushing the distribution toward a standard Gaussian, the latent space has useful structure:</p>

    <ul>
      <li style="position: relative;"><strong>Nearby points</strong> in latent space map to <strong>similar images</strong> after decoding</li>
      <li style="position: relative;"><strong>Interpolation</strong> between two latent codes produces a smooth visual transition, not a jarring pixel-space average</li>
      <li style="position: relative;">The space is <strong>dense</strong>: most random samples from <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">N</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0, I)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.1474em;">N</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord">0</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.0785em;">I</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span></span> decode to plausible-looking images (not noise or artifacts)</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="position: relative;">This last property is exactly why the VAE latent space is a good place to run diffusion. The diffusion model's job at the end of sampling is to produce a clean latent code <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.5806em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.044em;">z</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span class="" style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.044em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">0</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span class=""></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>, and the VAE decoder can turn any reasonable latent code into a good image.</p>

    <!-- Section 3: Latent Diffusion -->
    <h2 id="latent-diffusion" style="position: relative;"><span class="section-number">10.3</span> Latent Diffusion — The Key Insight</h2>

    <p style="position: relative;">The key idea (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.10752" target="_blank">Rombach et al., 2022</a>): instead of running diffusion in pixel space, <strong>run it in the VAE's latent space</strong>. The recipe has three steps:</p>

    <div class="algorithm">
      <div class="algorithm-title">The Latent Diffusion Recipe</div>
      <ol>
        <li style="position: relative;"><strong>Train a VAE</strong> on images—learn encoder <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.0576em;">E</span></span></span></span></span> and decoder <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.0278em;">D</span></span></span></span></span>. Then <em>freeze it completely</em>.</li>
        <li style="position: relative;"><strong>Encode all training images:</strong> <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_0 = E(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.5806em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.044em;">z</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span class="" style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.044em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">0</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span class=""></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.0576em;">E</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span></span>—you now have a dataset of latent codes instead of pixel images.</li>
        <li style="position: relative;"><strong>Train a diffusion model</strong> on <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.5806em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.044em;">z</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span class="" style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.044em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">0</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span class=""></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> instead of <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span></span></span></span></span>. The U-Net is smaller because it operates on <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64 \times 64 \times 4</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">64</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">64</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">4</span></span></span></span></span> latents instead of <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>512</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>512</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512 \times 512 \times 3</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">512</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">512</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">3</span></span></span></span></span> pixels.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <p style="position: relative;">At inference, the process runs in reverse:</p>

    <ol>
      <li style="position: relative;">Sample <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="script">N</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_T \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.5806em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.044em;">z</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3283em;"><span class="" style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.044em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.1389em;">T</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span class=""></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">∼</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.1474em;">N</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord">0</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.0785em;">I</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span></span> in <strong>latent space</strong>—shape <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mn>64</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mn>64</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1, 4, 64, 64)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord">1</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord">4</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord">64</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord">64</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span></span></li>
      <li style="position: relative;">Denoise: <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>→</mo><mo>⋯</mo><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_T \to z_{T-1} \to \cdots \to z_0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.5806em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.044em;">z</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3283em;"><span class="" style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.044em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.1389em;">T</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span class=""></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">→</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6389em; vertical-align: -0.2083em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.044em;">z</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3283em;"><span class="" style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.044em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.1389em;">T</span><span class="mbin mtight">−</span><span class="mord mtight">1</span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2083em;"><span class=""></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">→</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.3669em;"></span><span class="minner">⋯</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">→</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.5806em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.044em;">z</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span class="" style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.044em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">0</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span class=""></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> using the diffusion model (same DDPM/DDIM sampling we learned in Lectures 3 and 7)</li>
      <li style="position: relative;">Decode: <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x = D(z_0)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.0278em;">D</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.044em;">z</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span class="" style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.044em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">0</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span class=""></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span></span>—back to pixel space, shape <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mn>512</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mn>512</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1, 3, 512, 512)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord">1</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord">3</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord">512</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord">512</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span></span></li>
    </ol>

    <h3 id="why-it-works" style="position: relative;">Why This Works So Well</h3>

    <p style="position: relative;">The two stages <strong>specialize</strong> in different things:</p>

    <ul>
      <li style="position: relative;">The <strong>VAE handles perceptual compression</strong>: it strips away imperceptible high-frequency detail that humans don't notice. The pixel-level reconstruction doesn't need to be perfect; it just needs to look right.</li>
      <li style="position: relative;">The <strong>diffusion model handles semantic generation</strong>: it only needs to model the meaningful structure of images (shapes, textures, compositions), not every individual pixel.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="position: relative;">The compression from <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>512</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>512</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512 \times 512 \times 3</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">512</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">512</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">3</span></span></span></span></span> to <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64 \times 64 \times 4</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">64</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">64</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">4</span></span></span></span></span> is a <strong>48× reduction</strong> in the number of values. The U-Net is proportionally smaller, attention is affordable (operating on <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">,</mo><mn>096</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64 \times 64 = 4{,}096</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">64</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">64</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8389em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord">4</span><span class="mord"><span class="mpunct">,</span></span><span class="mord">096</span></span></span></span></span> tokens instead of <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>512</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>512</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>262</mn><mo separator="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">,</mo><mn>144</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512 \times 512 = 262{,}144</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">512</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">512</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8389em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord">262</span><span class="mord"><span class="mpunct">,</span></span><span class="mord">144</span></span></span></span></span>), and training is <strong>10–100× cheaper</strong> than pixel-space diffusion at the same resolution.</p>

    <h3 id="latent-pseudocode" style="position: relative;">Pseudocode</h3>

    <div class="pseudocode">
<span class="comment"># Stage 1: Train VAE (done once, then frozen)</span>
vae = <span class="function">train_vae</span>(image_dataset)

<span class="comment"># Stage 2: Encode all training images into latent space</span>
latent_dataset = [vae.<span class="function">encode</span>(x) <span class="keyword">for</span> x <span class="keyword">in</span> image_dataset]

<span class="comment"># Stage 3: Train diffusion model on latents</span>
<span class="comment"># Exactly the same training loop as Lecture 6, but on z_0 instead of x</span>
diffusion_model = <span class="function">train_diffusion</span>(latent_dataset)

<span class="comment"># Inference: generate new images</span>
z_T = torch.<span class="function">randn</span>(batch_size, <span class="number">4</span>, <span class="number">64</span>, <span class="number">64</span>)  <span class="comment"># start from noise in latent space</span>
z_0 = <span class="function">ddim_sample</span>(diffusion_model, z_T, schedule, num_steps=<span class="number">50</span>)
images = vae.<span class="function">decode</span>(z_0)  <span class="comment"># decode back to pixel space: (B, 3, 512, 512)</span>
    </div>

    <p style="position: relative;">Notice how little changes from the pixel-space pipeline in Lectures 3–7. The forward process, training loop, and sampling algorithm are <em>identical</em>; we've just swapped the data from pixels to latents.</p>

    <!-- Section 4: Stable Diffusion Architecture -->
    <h2 id="stable-diffusion" style="position: relative;"><span class="section-number">10.4</span> The Stable Diffusion Architecture</h2>

    <p style="position: relative;">Stable Diffusion (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.10752" target="_blank">Rombach et al., 2022</a>) is latent diffusion plus text conditioning. The architecture has three main components, each trained separately:</p>

    <h3 id="component-vae" style="position: relative;">Component 1: The VAE</h3>

    <p style="position: relative;">A KL-regularized autoencoder that handles the pixel↔latent conversion:</p>

    <ul>
      <li style="position: relative;"><strong>Encoder:</strong> <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>512</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>512</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>→</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512 \times 512 \times 3 \to 64 \times 64 \times 4</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">512</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">512</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">3</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">→</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">64</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">64</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">4</span></span></span></span></span> (8× spatial compression, ~48× total)</li>
      <li style="position: relative;"><strong>Decoder:</strong> <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>→</mo><mn>512</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>512</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64 \times 64 \times 4 \to 512 \times 512 \times 3</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">64</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">64</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">4</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">→</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">512</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">512</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">3</span></span></span></span></span></li>
      <li style="position: relative;">Trained separately on large image datasets, then frozen</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="component-unet" style="position: relative;">Component 2: The U-Net</h3>

    <p style="position: relative;">The diffusion model operates entirely in the <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64 \times 64</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">64</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">64</span></span></span></span></span> latent space. It's the same U-Net architecture we built in Lecture 4, with one addition: <strong>cross-attention layers</strong> that let the model attend to text embeddings. At each attention block, the image features are queries, and the text embeddings are keys and values. This is how the model "listens" to the text prompt.</p>

    <p style="position: relative;">The U-Net is time-conditioned (sinusoidal embeddings, just like Lectures 4–5) and much smaller than a pixel-space model at the same output resolution would be.</p>

    <h3 id="component-text" style="position: relative;">Component 3: The Text Encoder</h3>

    <p style="position: relative;">Converts a text prompt into a sequence of embeddings that the U-Net can attend to:</p>

    <ul>
      <li style="position: relative;"><strong>SD 1.5:</strong> <a href="https://openai.com/research/clip" target="_blank">CLIP</a> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vision_transformer" target="_blank">ViT</a>-L/14—77 tokens, 768-dimensional embeddings</li>
      <li style="position: relative;"><strong>SDXL:</strong> dual text encoders (CLIP + OpenCLIP) for richer representations</li>
      <li style="position: relative;"><strong>SD 3:</strong> T5-XXL for more powerful text understanding</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="position: relative;">The text encoder is pretrained (on image-text pairs for CLIP, on text for T5) and frozen during diffusion training. Only the U-Net's weights are trained.</p>

    <h3 id="inference-pipeline" style="position: relative;">The Full Inference Pipeline</h3>

    <div class="algorithm">
      <div class="algorithm-title">Stable Diffusion Inference</div>
      <ol>
        <li style="position: relative;"><strong>Text encoding:</strong> prompt → text encoder → text embeddings with shape <span class="keyword">(seq_len, d_text)</span></li>
        <li style="position: relative;"><strong>Sample noise:</strong> <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="script">N</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_T \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.5806em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.044em;">z</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3283em;"><span class="" style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.044em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.1389em;">T</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span class=""></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">∼</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.1474em;">N</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord">0</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.0785em;">I</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span></span> with shape <span class="keyword">(1, 4, 64, 64)</span></li>
        <li style="position: relative;"><strong>Iterative denoising</strong> (e.g., 50 DDIM steps):
          <ul>
            <li style="position: relative;">U-Net predicts noise, conditioned on timestep <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6151em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span></span></span></span></span> and text embeddings (via cross-attention)</li>
            <li style="position: relative;">Classifier-free guidance (Lecture 9) combines conditional and unconditional predictions</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li style="position: relative;"><strong>Decode:</strong> <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x = D(z_0)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.0278em;">D</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.044em;">z</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span class="" style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.044em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">0</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span class=""></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span></span> → shape <span class="keyword">(1, 3, 512, 512)</span></li>
        <li style="position: relative;"><strong>Denormalize</strong> from <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1, 1]</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mopen">[</span><span class="mord">−</span><span class="mord">1</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord">1</span><span class="mclose">]</span></span></span></span></span> to <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mn>255</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0, 255]</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mopen">[</span><span class="mord">0</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord">255</span><span class="mclose">]</span></span></span></span></span> and display</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <p style="position: relative;">Every component we've studied in this course shows up: the U-Net (Lecture 4), noise schedules (Lecture 5), the training loop (Lecture 6), DDIM sampling (Lecture 7), and classifier-free guidance (Lecture 9). Latent diffusion ties them all together into a system that actually works at scale.</p>

    <!-- Section 5: SD Evolution -->
    <h2 id="sd-evolution" style="position: relative;"><span class="section-number">10.5</span> The Evolution of Stable Diffusion</h2>

    <p style="position: relative;">Stable Diffusion has evolved rapidly since its release, with each version improving text encoders, model capacity, and the underlying framework:</p>

    <ul>
      <li style="position: relative;"><strong>SD 1.5</strong> — the original release. CLIP text encoder, <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64 \times 64</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">64</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">64</span></span></span></span></span> latent, ~860M U-Net parameters. <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">ε</span></span></span></span></span>-prediction with a linear noise schedule. The first widely adopted open-source image generation model.</li>
      <li style="position: relative;"><strong>SD 2.x</strong> — switched to OpenCLIP text encoder, adopted <strong>v-prediction</strong> (we'll cover this in Lecture 11), larger model. Better image quality, especially at higher resolutions.</li>
      <li style="position: relative;"><strong>SDXL</strong> — dual text encoders for richer text understanding, <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>128</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128 \times 128</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7278em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord">128</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">×</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">128</span></span></span></span></span> base latent for higher fidelity, plus a separate refiner model. Significantly better prompt adherence.</li>
      <li style="position: relative;"><strong>SD 3</strong> — a major architecture shift: replaces the U-Net with a <strong>Diffusion Transformer</strong> (Lecture 12), uses T5-XXL text encoder, and adopts <strong>flow matching</strong> (Lecture 13) instead of DDPM. The state of the art.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout insight" style="position: relative;">
      <div class="callout-label" style="position: relative;">Key Insight</div>
      The trend across SD versions is clear: better text encoders, larger models, and more advanced training frameworks. But the core latent diffusion insight—compress with a VAE, diffuse in latent space—remains unchanged.
    </div>

    <!-- Section 6: Looking Ahead -->
    <h2 id="looking-ahead" style="position: relative;"><span class="section-number">10.6</span> Looking Ahead</h2>

    <p style="position: relative;">Latent diffusion solves the resolution problem, but we've been using <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">ε</span></span></span></span></span>-prediction throughout this course without questioning it. Is predicting the noise always the best choice? And are there better noise schedules than the linear and cosine schedules we've seen?</p>

    <p style="position: relative;">In the next lecture, we'll explore <strong>prediction targets</strong>—three different ways to parameterize what the model predicts—and <strong>advanced noise schedules</strong> that fix subtle training-inference mismatches. These are the refinements that separate a working model from a production model.</p>

  



